## Sizing a Men's White Gold Diamond Ring

Men's rings run wider than women's. A wide band requires a half size up from your standard ring size.

The rule: order a half size up for any band 6mm or wider. The extra width reduces how far the ring slides onto your finger. For narrower solitaire shanks (4mm and under), your standard size applies.

One practical tip: if you are between sizes, go with the larger of the two. A ring that is slightly loose is easier to live with than one that is tight.

A ring sizer from any jeweler gives you an accurate measurement before you order. Some jewelers offer free sizing at the same rhodium replating appointment — worth asking.

Measure your finger at the end of the day. Fingers swell in warm weather and after activity. A ring sized in the morning may feel tight by evening.

For channel-set rings, fit matters more than for solitaires. A tight channel band is uncomfortable across its full width.

## Caring for a Men's White Gold Diamond Ring

White gold requires more maintenance than yellow or rose gold. The basic care routine is simple. Soap, lukewarm water, and a soft cloth are enough for weekly cleaning.

Avoid ultrasonic cleaners for prong-set diamonds. Vibrations can loosen stones in prong settings. Channel-set rings are more resistant but still benefit from manual cleaning.

Do not use toothpaste on white gold. Despite common advice, toothpaste contains mild abrasives that scratch rhodium and speed up wear. A jeweler's polishing cloth is the right tool for quick surface maintenance.

It is available at any jewelry supply store for under $10. It costs less than 1% of a rhodium replating visit.

Bring the ring to a jeweler every 2–3 years for rhodium replating and channel inspection. The channel walls can loosen with daily impact wear. Catching it early prevents stone loss.

This appointment is part of the Rhodium Reset Cycle budget you planned at purchase. It is not an extra cost — it is the schedule.

